From bc67ee012ff6da73d262701d75ee7b02c91beb98 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 12:45:37 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] fix(kanban): stale checks count only awake time, from the
 run's own start [daveey]

A Mac lid-close longer than an hour made the first dispatcher pass after wake
SIGTERM every live host-local worker: release_stale_claims' heartbeat backstop
(60 min), detect_stale_running and enforce_max_runtime all measured wall-clock
time, so the sleep read as worker silence (44 kills since 10-22, 8 cards gave up).

- hermes_cli/kanban_suspend_ledger.py: each dispatcher pass stamps kanban.db;
  a gap over max(300, 3 x kanban.dispatch_interval_seconds) is recorded as a
  suspend (pruned after 7 d). awake_seconds() subtracts recorded suspends.
- _run_reclaim_phase records the pass before any stale check.
- release_stale_claims / detect_stale_running measure heartbeat age in awake
  time from max(last_heartbeat_at, active run started_at), so a fresh run is
  never stale against its predecessor's heartbeat; enforce_max_runtime and the
  stale sweep's elapsed use awake time too. Thresholds and TTLs unchanged.
  An empty ledger (always-on hub) keeps the old wall-clock behaviour.

Size caps: _extend_live_stale_claim and _defer_reclaim_for_live_worker move
unchanged into hermes_cli/kanban_db_stale_claims.py (re-exported).
